        "t1": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "text": "<p><strong>What South Carolina homeowners build with glass</strong></p><p>The Lowcountry sets the tone. Charleston, Mount Pleasant, Kiawah, Hilton Head and Bluffton are full of houses raised on pilings, with piazzas and upper porches facing marsh or ocean. A glass balcony railing there keeps the water in view and shrugs off salt air. Up the coast, the Grand Strand around Myrtle Beach, Murrells Inlet and Pawleys Island mixes beach houses with condo balconies.</p><p>Inland, the Midlands are lake country. Homeowners in Lexington, Chapin and Irmo build multi-level decks on Lake Murray. A glass deck railing lets them watch the water from the kitchen. See the <a href=\"/pages/south-carolina-glass-railing-columbia\">glass railings in Columbia</a> page for that market. The Upstate adds Greenville, Spartanburg and Anderson, with Lake Keowee and Lake Hartwell homes and hillside decks looking toward the Blue Ridge foothills. Rock Hill and Fort Mill, near Charlotte, are new-build territory with open staircases and glass stair railing.</p>"
          }
        },
        "t2": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "text": "<p><strong>South Carolina code in short</strong></p><p>South Carolina adopts its codes statewide through the South Carolina Building Codes Council. The current editions are the 2021 International Residential Code and International Building Code. The South Carolina code sets guards at 36 inches minimum for houses and 42 inches for commercial and multifamily buildings, including condo balconies. For pools, South Carolina applies the International Swimming Pool and Spa Code, and section 305 sets a 48-inch barrier. Our 46-inch panel in spigots gives that 48-inch height.</p><p>On the coast, the hurricane wind zones mean the building department may ask how the spigots are attached. Our engineering documents, with testing results and blocking details for wood and concrete, cover top-mount and side-mount spigots. Permits are issued by the city or county, so check with your local building department before you order glass. Read the full summary on our <a href=\"/pages/south-carolina-building-code-for-glass-railings\">South Carolina building code for glass railings</a> page.</p>"
          }
        },
        "t3": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "text": "<p><strong>Delivery to any South Carolina address</strong></p><p>Orders ship from our warehouse in Quebec and clear the border before they reach you. The price at checkout is final, with no duties, tariffs or brokerage charges added later. Standard orders are processed in 2 to 3 business days, then delivered by freight carrier in 5 to 10 business days. The truck has a liftgate, so the crate is lowered to the driveway, whether that is on Sullivan's Island or in Simpsonville. Custom glass takes 2 to 3 weeks of shipping after processing.</p><p>Orders over 3,000 USD ship free; the <a href=\"/pages/free-shipping-information\">free shipping page</a> has the details. Inspect the crate on arrival and report any damage within 3 days and before installation. We replace or refund the damaged item at no cost.</p>"
          }
        }
